Here's the TikZ diagram of a block on a 30-degree incline with the requested forces:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Grid
    \foreach \x in {-1,-0.5,...,3} {
        \draw[gray!20, very thin] (\x,-1) -- (\x,1);
    }
    \foreach \y in {-1,-0.5,...,1} {
        \draw[gray!20, very thin] (-1,\y) -- (3,\y);
    }

    % Incline
    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3,0) node[right, yshift=0.4cm] {Ground};
    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,2) node[above, xshift=0.4cm] {30° Incline};

    % Block
    \draw[fill=blue!20] (1,0.5) rectangle (2,1.5) node[midway] {Block};

    % Forces
    \draw[->, ultra thick, red] (1.5,1) -- (1.5,0) node[left, yshift=0.4cm] {$\mathbf{F_g}$};
    \draw[->, ultra thick, green] (1.5,1) -- (1.2,1.4) node[above, xshift=0.4cm] {$\mathbf{F_N}$};

    % Angle
    \draw[->] (0.5,0) arc (0:30:0.5) node[midway, right] {30°};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This diagram shows:
1. A 30-degree incline with a block on it
2. The gravity vector (Fg) pointing straight down
3. The normal force (FN) perpendicular to the surface
4. The angle of the incline clearly marked

The forces are color-coded: red for gravity and green for the normal force. The block is shown as a blue rectangle on the incline.

Here's the TikZ diagram of a unit circle with the standard angles and the 45-degree projections:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Unit Circle
    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.5cm);

    % Axes
    \draw[->, thick] (-1.7,0) -- (1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, thick] (0,-1.7) -- (0,1.7) node[above] {$y$};

    % Standard Angles
    \foreach \angle/\label in {30/30°, 45/45°, 60/60°} {
        \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (\angle:1.5);
        \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (\angle:1.6) node[above right] {\label};
    }

    % 45° Projections
    \draw[dashed] (45:1.5) -- (45:1.5 |- 0,0) node[below] {$\cos(45°)$};
    \draw[dashed] (45:1.5) -- (45:1.5 -| 0,0) node[left] {$\sin(45°)$};

    % Points
    \filldraw (45:1.5) circle (1pt) node[above right] {$(x,y)$};
    \filldraw (45:1.5 |- 0,0) circle (1pt);
    \filldraw (45:1.5 -| 0,0) circle (1pt);

    % Labels
    \node at (0.5,1.2) {$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$};
    \node at (1.2,0.5) {$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This diagram shows:
1. A unit circle with radius 1
2. The standard angles of 30°, 45°, and 60° marked
3. The 45° angle with its sine and cosine projections clearly shown
4. The coordinates of the point on the circle at 45°
5. The values of sin(45°) and cos(45°) as $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$

Here's a detailed graph of the inverse sine function \( y = \arcsin(x) \) with clearly marked domain and range:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.3, transform shape]
    \tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize}}

    % Axes
    \draw[->, thick] (-1.5,0) -- (1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,2) node[above] {$y$};

    % Grid
    \foreach \x in {-1,...,1} {
        \draw[gray!20, very thin] (\x,-0.5) -- (\x,2);
    }
    \foreach \y in {0,...,2} {
        \draw[gray!20, very thin] (-1.5,\y) -- (1.5,\y);
    }

    % Domain and Range
    \draw[<->, thick, blue] (-1,0) -- (1,0) node[midway, below] {Domain: $[-1, 1]$};
    \draw[<->, thick, blue] (1.5,0) -- (1.5,1.57) node[midway, right] {Range: $[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$};

    % Function
    \draw[domain=-1:1, smooth, variable=\x, thick, red] plot ({\x}, {rad(asin(\x))});

    % Points
    \filldraw (-1,1.57) circle (1pt) node[left] {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
    \filldraw (0,0) circle (1pt) node[below left] {$0$};
    \filldraw (1,1.57) circle (1pt) node[right] {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};

    % Labels
    \node[red] at (0.5,1.5) {$y = \arcsin(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This graph shows:
1. The inverse sine function \( y = \arcsin(x) \) plotted in red
2. The domain of the function marked as $[-1, 1]$ on the x-axis
3. The range of the function marked as $[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]$ on the y-axis
4. Key points at $x = -1$, $x = 0$, and $x = 1$ with their corresponding y-values
5. The function is plotted over its entire domain with a smooth curve
